WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Every mobile application translates abstract code logic into the visual reality the user interacts with on their device.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.700
In React Native, this bridge is a surface. It's a one-to-one mapping, where one entry point renders into a specific native view.

3
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:21.800
Applications can scale this, rendering multiple surfaces simultaneously from one JavaScript bundle.

4
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:26.000
Each maintains its own node tree, props, and React state.

5
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:32.200
These surfaces appear as separate elements on the screen, but they share the same underlying architecture.

6
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:41.400
Assuming these surfaces are fully isolated is a mistake that leads to unexpected behavior when the different component trees attempt to access shared resources.

7
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:50.000
Managing multi-surface architecture requires looking past the visual independence of the UI to understand how they are connected at the system level.

8
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.600
To understand those connections, we have to look at how the app handles multiple surfaces during execution.

9
00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:02.400
The architectural rule is that multiple surfaces originating from the same bundle are never isolated runtimes.

10
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.000
Even visually separate active surfaces route operations through one JavaScript thread and engine.

11
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.700
They share a module registry, making dependencies common across every surface.

12
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:21.800
On Android, each surface is a fragment backed by React surface. On iOS, they mount within a UI view controller connected to RCT host.

13
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:29.200
The native operating system provides separate containers for the UI, but those containers are still tied to the same shared runtime.

14
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:35.100
This creates an environment where one surface can interfere with another, despite appearing completely separate.

15
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:41.100
This shared thread model reaches its limit in super app ecosystems that require strict boundaries between features.

16
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:48.900
For example, a surface handling sensitive payment processing should not have access to the same memory or modules as third-party advertising code.

17
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:55.000
Different engineering teams may need to ship bundles that rely on conflicting versions of the same dependency.

18
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.300
To solve this, these scenarios require React Native sandbox.

19
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:04.700
Sandboxing changes the architecture by creating separate module registries for different surfaces.

20
00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:11.500
This forces the creation of independent JavaScript engines, ensuring the surfaces do not share state or memory.

21
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.400
Because the registries are no longer shared, synchronous data transfer is impossible.

22
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:22.000
All inter sandbox communication must instead use an asynchronous post message API.

23
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:30.400
True security and team autonomy require moving away from the default shared thread model and adopting the constraints of an isolated environment.

24
00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:38.600
Beyond memory architecture, we have to manage the timing of the surface lifecycle, specifically the moments before a surface is ready to render.

25
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:46.300
This is the initialization tax. Before the UI appears, the Hermes engine must load and parse the entire JavaScript bundle.

26
00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:51.700
On lower end devices, this parsing sequence can take up to two seconds, leaving the user waiting.

27
00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:55.400
Engineers use warm start strategies to mitigate this delay.

28
00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:00.400
The first strategy, eager host start, initializes the host when the app boots.

29
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:06.700
This removes delay, but consumes 10 megabytes of RAM, even if the user never opens a React Native surface.

30
00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:11.300
A conditional warm start waits for a trigger, like logging in before starting.

31
00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:16.300
This saves memory for inactive users, but introduces a slight delay during activation.

32
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:20.700
A pre-built surface triggers the start command just before the user transitions.

33
00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.300
It renders invisibly in the background, ready to display the moment the transition occurs.

34
00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:30.500
Finally, a RAM bundle with inline requires changes how the bundler packages code.

35
00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:35.200
Instead of parsing up front, the engine lazily loads modules only when needed.

36
00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:41.100
Managing the host requires balancing the need for immediate UI response against the memory limits of the device.

37
00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:48.400
When the architecture and the lifecycle are managed correctly, they create a fluid final experience for the user.

38
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.500
A surface serves as the bridge between a native view and the JavaScript engine.

39
00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:58.000
By default, these surfaces are connected by a shared thread and registry.

40
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:04.900
When the app requires strict security or independent dependencies, sandboxing is used to create hard boundaries.

41
00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.900
And to ensure the UI feels responsive, we use warm start strategies

42
00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.100
to account for the initialization time of the engine.

43
00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:17.600
Building complex multi-surface applications requires moving beyond default assumptions

44
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:22.100
to actively engineer the memory boundaries and the startup lifecycle.

